⟦- if .Profile.FullAuth⟧
| Passkeys, Google, Apple and GitHub | `usecase/passkeys.go`, `usecase/social.go` |
⟦- end⟧
| Routes and their guards | `delivery/routes.go` |
| Tables | `repository/migrations/`, the same SQL as the `*_auth*.sql` files in `db/migrations` |

`cmd/api/main.go` builds it with `authhttp.New(...)`, imported from `internal/modules/auth`: the options and hooks work as the library's do. `gorbital.lock` records the library version it was copied from. From then on library releases don't change it: run `orb doctor`, which says when the library's `authhttp` has changed since and quotes the changelog entries, then port the fixes you want.⟦if .Profile.Named⟧ The same goes for ⟦.Profile.Vocabulary.Plural⟧: `internal/modules/orgs` holds ⟦.Profile.Vocabulary.Plural⟧, members and roles (`usecase/orgs.go`, `usecase/members.go`, `usecase/roles.go`), invitations (`usecase/invitations.go`) and the `orgs_purge` job, copied from `orgshttp` and built in `main.go` with `orgshttp.Module(auth)`.⟦end⟧

⟦end⟧⟦if .Profile.Named⟧## ⟦.Profile.ScopeTitle⟧

Data belongs to ⟦.Profile.Vocabulary.Plural⟧, and people reach it through their role in each one. Every account gets a **personal workspace** (an ⟦.Profile.Vocabulary.Name⟧ with one owner that can't take invitations) when it is created; teams create ⟦.Profile.Vocabulary.Plural⟧. Organisations, members and invitations are the module in `internal/modules/orgs`, added in `main.go` with `orgshttp.Module(auth)`.

```bash
ORG=$(curl -s -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1/⟦.Profile.Vocabulary.Segment⟧ -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{"name":"Acme"}' | jq -r .id)                 # you become its owner
curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1/⟦.Profile.Vocabulary.Segment⟧/$ORG/invitations -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{"email":"sam@example.com","role":"admin"}'  # Sam gets an email with a link (see the Dev Portal's Mail screen)
curl http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1/⟦.Profile.Vocabulary.Segment⟧/$ORG/projects -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN"
```

| Rule | Behaviour |
|---|---|
| Roles | `owner`, `admin` or `member`, one per person. A module grants its permissions to them with `OrgRoles` in its `module.go`. Nobody gives or changes a role granting a permission their own role doesn't, and only owners manage owners |
| Outsiders | Every `/v1/⟦.Profile.Vocabulary.Segment⟧/{⟦.Profile.Vocabulary.PathParam⟧}/…` request by someone who isn't a member answers 404 `⟦.Profile.Vocabulary.NotFoundCode⟧`, so ⟦.Profile.Vocabulary.Name⟧ IDs can't be probed |
| Invitations | The link opens the page in the `orgs.invitation_url` runtime setting with `#token=…`; the page posts the token to `POST /v1/invitations/accept`. Accepting needs a signed-in account whose verified email is the invited address, while the inviter is still a member who may give the role. Inviting needs a verified address. Links last `orgs.invitation_ttl` (7 days); an ⟦.Profile.Vocabulary.Name⟧ sends at most 20 an hour, and a user `orgs.user_invitations_per_hour` (50) across ⟦.Profile.Vocabulary.Plural⟧ |
| Owners | An ⟦.Profile.Vocabulary.Name⟧ always has an owner: the last owner can't leave or be demoted, and can't delete their account while others are members (409 `sole_owner` lists the ⟦.Profile.Vocabulary.Plural⟧) |
| Creating | Needs a verified address; a user owns at most `orgs.max_owned` (20) ⟦.Profile.Vocabulary.Plural⟧ besides their personal workspace |
| Deleting | Members lose access at once; an owner can `POST /v1/⟦.Profile.Vocabulary.Segment⟧/{⟦.Profile.Vocabulary.PathParam⟧}/restore` until `orgs.deleted_org_retention` (30 days) ends, when the `orgs_purge` job removes it with its data |
| Platform staff | `platform_admin` and `ops_viewer` don't see ⟦.Profile.Vocabulary.Plural⟧' data |

Set where invitation links go before inviting real people:

```bash
curl -X PUT http://127.0.0.1:8080/ops/settings/orgs.invitation_url -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{"value":"https://app.example.com/invitations","version":0,"reason":"frontend invitation page"}'
```

⟦else if .Profile.CustomScope⟧## ⟦.Profile.ScopeTitle⟧

Data belongs to ⟦.Profile.Vocabulary.Plural⟧, and this app decides who belongs to one. `internal/modules/scope` holds the tables (`⟦.Profile.ScopeTable⟧`, `⟦.Profile.ScopeMembersTable⟧`) and the `gorbital.ScopeAuthorizer` every `/v1/⟦.Profile.Vocabulary.Segment⟧/{⟦.Profile.Vocabulary.PathParam⟧}/…` route asks through `guard.Scope`. Its `AuthorizeScope` returns `gorbital.ErrNotImplemented` until you write it, and `internal/modules/scope/authorizer_test.go` fails until you do — on purpose: the rule is yours, and no filter is put in front of your data that you didn't write.

| Rule | Where |
|---|---|
| Who is a member, and with which role | `internal/modules/scope/authorizer.go`, `AuthorizeScope` |
| What a ⟦.Profile.Vocabulary.Name⟧ ID looks like | `Scope().ValidID` |
| The roles a module's permissions name | `Scope().Roles`, and `ScopeRoles` in each module's `module.go` |
| The refusal a non-member gets | 404 `⟦.Profile.Vocabulary.NotFoundCode⟧`, so IDs can't be probed |
| The ⟦.Profile.Vocabulary.Name⟧ each database connection carries | `Scope().Session`, for row-level security |

⟦end⟧## Configuration

Two layers:

| Layer | Holds | Change it |
|---|---|---|
| Environment ([.env.example](.env.example)) | Secrets and infrastructure: database URL, tokens, the Resend API key or SMTP login, addresses | Edit env and restart |
| Runtime settings and job definitions | Tunables such as the ping reply, the email sender name and address, job schedules, timeouts and retries | `PUT /ops/settings/{key}`, `PUT /ops/jobs/definitions/{name}`; live on every instance |

```bash
curl -X PUT http://127.0.0.1:8080/ops/settings/example.ping_message \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{"value":"hello","version":0,"reason":"demo"}'
```

## Email

This app sends email with **Resend** (`cmd/api/mail.go`). In development every email goes to orb dev's mail catcher instead (the Dev Portal's Mail screen), so no key is needed to start.

1. For real email, create a key at https://resend.com/api-keys and set `RESEND_API_KEY` in `.env`.
2. Set the sender: `PUT /ops/settings/mail.from_email` and `PUT /ops/settings/mail.from_name`.
3. Check it: `POST /ops/mail/test` with `{"to":"you@example.com"}`.

Prefer SMTP (Amazon SES, Postmark, Mailgun, your own server)? Run `orb add mail` and choose it. See the gorbital email guide for providers and delivery.

